==English==

===Etymology===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*kʷ-}}
From {{inh|en|enm|who}}, {{m|enm|hwo}}, {{m|enm|huo}}, {{m|enm|wha}}, {{m|enm|hwoa}}, {{m|enm|hwa}}, from {{inh|en|ang|hwā}} (dative {{m|ang|hwām}}, genitive {{m|ang|hwæs}}), from {{inh|en|gmw-pro|*hwaʀ}}, from {{inh|en|gem-pro|*hwaz}}, from {{der|en|ine-pro|*kʷos}}, {{m|ine-pro|*kʷís}}.

The sound change {{IPAchar|/hw/}} > {{IPAchar|/h/}} (without a corresponding change in spelling) was due to [[w:Wh-cluster reductions|''wh''-cluster reduction]] after an irregular change of {{IPAchar|/ɑː/}} to {{IPAchar|/oː/}} in Middle English (instead of the expected {{IPAchar|/ɔː/}}) and further to {{IPAchar|/uː/}} regularly in Early Modern English. A similar change occurred in {{m|en|two}}. Compare {{m|en|how}}, which underwent ''wh''-reduction earlier (in Old English), and thus is spelt with ''h''.

Compare {{cog|sco|wha}}, {{cog|fy|wa}}, {{cog|nl|wie}}, {{cog|nds|we}}, {{cog|de|wer}}, {{cog|sv|vem}}, {{cog|da|hvem}}, {{cog|nb|hvem}}, {{cog|nn|kven}}, {{cog|is|hver}}.

